      "body": "> Just to make sure, the code you shared is what you were referring to on discord as well?\n\nYep\n\n> This MWE is a bit of an anti pattern though, you use the autodiff backend but never backpropagate anything. You're just visiting the model params in an infinite loop and collect some stats by calling `tensor.abs().max()`.\n> \n> The current cleanup strategy for `Autodiff` is called on `backward()`. So right now you're just increasingly keeping autodiff references in a loop.\n\nThe real-world example in which this came up was when not all of the model's parameters were touched by `backward()`, but were touched by this visitor, so those kept accumulating over the course of training. I suppose if that is the intended behavior then it is a bug in my code instead.\n\nIt seemed like autodiff should be able to tell when it's no longer possible to call `backward()` on a graph, but I don't know how difficult that would be to implement or if it would be worth it. Thanks for taking a look at this, and sorry if this is something that's already been discussed.",
