      "body": "> The real-world example in which this came up was when not all of the model's parameters were touched by backward(), but were touched by this visitor, so those kept accumulating over the course of training.\n\nAhhh I see. There is some logic to remove \"orphaned entries\" in the graph: https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/blob/main/crates/burn-autodiff/src/runtime/graph.rs\n\nLink to related discussion / changes: https://github.com/tracel-ai/burn/pull/4039\n\nBut this is only for unused roots. Parameters are persistent, they are part of the model and will always have a reference.\n\nFor visitors which are read-only operations that shouldn't impact the gradient graph, the idiomatic approach in is to drop down to the inner backend so the operations are not tracked by autodiff.\n\n```rust\nimpl<B: AutodiffBackend> ModuleVisitor<B> for SanityChecker {\n    fn visit_float<const D: usize>(&mut self, param: &Param<Tensor<B, D, Float>>) {\n        let tensor = param.val().inner();\n        let max = tensor.clone().abs().max().into_scalar().elem::<f32>();\n        if max > self.max_param {\n            self.max_param = max;\n        }\n        let mut prod = 1;\n        let dims = tensor.dims();\n        for i in 0..D {\n            prod *= dims[i];\n        }\n        self.param_count += prod;\n    }\n```\n\nNote that this requires the implementation to be generic over `AutodiffBackend` instead to call `.inner()` explicitly. I believe that should fix your issue.\n\nSee also the parameter clamping example in the book: https://burn.dev/books/burn/building-blocks/module.html#visitor--mapper",
